1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,959
The chip inside a recent iPhone, Apple's A17 Pro,

2
00:00:03,959 --> 00:00:07,020
holds about nineteen billion transistors.

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:12,544
Each one is a microscopic switch, flicking on and off billions of times a second.

4
00:00:12,544 --> 00:00:15,707
Together, they run everything you do on that phone.

5
00:00:16,207 --> 00:00:18,977
Now imagine the transistor was never invented.

6
00:00:18,977 --> 00:00:22,950
Every one of those switches has to be a glowing glass vacuum tube.

7
00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:24,817
What does your world look like?

8
00:00:29,067 --> 00:00:30,189
First, the rules.

9
00:00:30,189 --> 00:00:32,963
Everything else in history stays the same.

10
00:00:32,963 --> 00:00:35,274
Electricity, radio, the light bulb.

11
00:00:35,274 --> 00:00:39,236
We just never learn to make a switch out of a solid crystal.

12
00:00:39,736 --> 00:00:42,113
So we're stuck with the vacuum tube.

13
00:00:42,113 --> 00:00:44,887
It's a glass bulb with the air pumped out.

14
00:00:44,887 --> 00:00:48,784
Inside, a hot filament boils electrons off a metal surface,

15
00:00:48,784 --> 00:00:51,955
and they fly across the vacuum to a metal plate.

16
00:00:52,455 --> 00:00:55,648
In between sits a fine wire mesh, called a grid.

17
00:00:55,648 --> 00:01:00,372
Put a small voltage on the grid, and you control a much bigger current.

18
00:01:00,372 --> 00:01:01,703
That's an amplifier.

19
00:01:01,703 --> 00:01:02,501
Or a switch.

20
00:01:02,501 --> 00:01:04,963
The same job a transistor does today.

21
00:01:05,463 --> 00:01:09,313
The first tube that could amplify like this, the Audion,

22
00:01:09,313 --> 00:01:11,925
was invented by Lee de Forest in 1906.

23
00:01:11,925 --> 00:01:16,463
It made long-distance phone calls and radio broadcasting possible.

24
00:01:16,463 --> 00:01:20,932
So in our world, the twentieth century still crackles with radio.

25
00:01:23,667 --> 00:01:24,942
During World War II,

26
00:01:24,942 --> 00:01:32,846
the Colossus machines at Bletchley Park in Britain used about two and a half thousand tubes each to help crack German codes.

27
00:01:33,346 --> 00:01:37,250
And for a while, that really is how computers were built.

28
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:38,415
In 1946, the U.S.

29
00:01:38,415 --> 00:01:42,525
Army's ENIAC was unveiled at the University of Pennsylvania.

30
00:01:42,525 --> 00:01:46,430
It had around seventeen and a half thousand vacuum tubes,

31
00:01:46,430 --> 00:01:49,444
and about five million hand-soldered joints.

32
00:01:49,944 --> 00:01:54,514
It weighed about thirty tons, and drew around a hundred and fifty kilowatts.

33
00:01:54,514 --> 00:01:58,904
That's like a hundred electric kettles boiling at the same time, all day.

34
00:01:59,404 --> 00:02:03,276
In return, it could do about five thousand additions a second.

35
00:02:03,276 --> 00:02:05,587
Its first programmers were six women,

36
00:02:05,587 --> 00:02:08,647
who learned the machine from its wiring diagrams,

37
00:02:08,647 --> 00:02:12,769
and programmed it by plugging cables and setting switches by hand.

38
00:02:13,633 --> 00:02:15,338
There was one big problem.

39
00:02:15,338 --> 00:02:17,831
Tubes burn out, just like light bulbs.

40
00:02:17,831 --> 00:02:21,241
Early on, ENIAC lost several tubes almost every day,

41
00:02:21,241 --> 00:02:23,995
and was out of action about half the time.

42
00:02:24,495 --> 00:02:28,510
Most failures happened while the tubes were warming up or cooling down,

43
00:02:28,510 --> 00:02:32,185
so the trick was to switch the machine off as little as possible.

44
00:02:32,685 --> 00:02:36,680
Even with sturdier tubes, one still failed about every two days.

45
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:40,051
Engineers became experts at hunting down the dead one.

46
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:44,132
Five years later, UNIVAC I went to work at the U.S.

47
00:02:44,132 --> 00:02:45,019
Census Bureau.

48
00:02:45,019 --> 00:02:48,314
It was one of the first computers sold commercially,

49
00:02:48,314 --> 00:02:51,229
and it still needed about five thousand tubes.

50
00:02:51,729 --> 00:02:55,402
Computers in this world are rare, enormous and expensive.

51
00:02:55,402 --> 00:02:59,848
And each one needs a room, a power line, and a team to keep it alive.

52
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:06,694
In our real history, everything changed in December 1947.

53
00:03:06,694 --> 00:03:08,634
At Bell Labs in New Jersey,

54
00:03:08,634 --> 00:03:14,668
John Bardeen and Walter Brattain pressed two gold contacts onto a slab of germanium,

55
00:03:14,668 --> 00:03:17,182
and the crystal amplified a signal.

56
00:03:17,682 --> 00:03:19,391
It was the first transistor.

57
00:03:19,391 --> 00:03:22,871
They showed it to their bosses two days before Christmas.

58
00:03:22,871 --> 00:03:23,359
Bardeen,

59
00:03:23,359 --> 00:03:28,059
Brattain and William Shockley later shared the Nobel Prize in Physics for it.

60
00:03:28,559 --> 00:03:29,501
That same year,

61
00:03:29,501 --> 00:03:33,769
engineers at Harvard found a moth stuck inside their relay computer,

62
00:03:33,769 --> 00:03:38,540
and taped it into the logbook as the first actual case of a bug being found.

63
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:42,031
In our what-if, that December day never comes.

64
00:03:42,031 --> 00:03:44,372
The germanium stays a lab curiosity.

65
00:03:44,372 --> 00:03:46,258
And the tube keeps its crown.

66
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,262
In the real 1950s, transistors spread fast.

67
00:03:52,262 --> 00:03:56,130
The first transistor radio went on sale in the U.S.

68
00:03:56,130 --> 00:04:00,226
in 1954, for forty-nine dollars and ninety-five cents.

69
00:04:00,226 --> 00:04:03,033
Music you could carry in your pocket.

70
00:04:03,533 --> 00:04:05,954
Hearing aids changed even faster.

71
00:04:05,954 --> 00:04:09,914
The first all-transistor hearing aid came out in 1953.

72
00:04:09,914 --> 00:04:15,269
A year later, ninety-seven percent of hearing aids used only transistors.

73
00:04:15,769 --> 00:04:18,428
In the tube world, none of that happens.

74
00:04:18,428 --> 00:04:22,417
Radios stay as big as a loaf of bread, need time to warm up,

75
00:04:22,417 --> 00:04:24,478
and live next to a wall socket.

76
00:04:24,478 --> 00:04:26,073
Hearing aids stay bulky.

77
00:04:28,833 --> 00:04:30,871
Then comes the race to the Moon.

78
00:04:30,871 --> 00:04:36,985
The real Apollo Guidance Computer was one of the first computers built from integrated circuits:

79
00:04:36,985 --> 00:04:42,144
about two thousand eight hundred tiny chips, each holding two simple logic gates.

80
00:04:42,644 --> 00:04:45,414
No transistors means no integrated circuits.

81
00:04:45,414 --> 00:04:54,291
A tube computer that could do the same job would be far heavier and hungrier for power than anything you'd want to squeeze into a spacecraft.

82
00:04:54,791 --> 00:04:57,053
Getting to the Moon wouldn't be impossible.

83
00:04:57,053 --> 00:04:58,578
But it would be a lot harder,

84
00:04:58,578 --> 00:05:01,839
and the ground crew would have to do far more of the thinking.

85
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:08,996
Now let's jump to today, and try to rebuild just one phone chip with tubes.

86
00:05:09,496 --> 00:05:13,929
ENIAC's power bill works out to roughly eight and a half watts per tube.

87
00:05:13,929 --> 00:05:16,392
Multiply that by nineteen billion tubes,

88
00:05:16,392 --> 00:05:19,470
and you need around a hundred and sixty gigawatts.

89
00:05:19,970 --> 00:05:23,641
That's in the range of a hundred and sixty large power plants.

90
00:05:23,641 --> 00:05:24,470
For one phone.

91
00:05:24,970 --> 00:05:25,280
Size?

92
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:28,385
Even a small tube is about the size of your thumb.

93
00:05:28,385 --> 00:05:33,788
Nineteen billion of them would fill roughly a hundred and fifty Olympic swimming pools.

94
00:05:34,288 --> 00:05:36,220
And at ENIAC's best failure rate,

95
00:05:36,220 --> 00:05:39,966
more than six of those tubes would burn out every single second.

96
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:44,235
So in the tube world, computers stay giant and rare.

97
00:05:44,235 --> 00:05:48,594
Governments and big companies own a few, in air-conditioned halls.

98
00:05:48,594 --> 00:05:50,113
Nobody has one at home.

99
00:05:50,613 --> 00:05:51,363
No laptops.

100
00:05:51,363 --> 00:05:52,385
No smartphones.

101
00:05:52,385 --> 00:05:56,610
Probably no internet as we know it, and no map in your pocket.

102
00:05:57,110 --> 00:05:59,537
Television and radio still exist.

103
00:05:59,537 --> 00:06:01,744
Tubes handled those just fine.

104
00:06:01,744 --> 00:06:03,510
But a pocket calculator?

105
00:06:03,510 --> 00:06:04,687
A digital watch?

106
00:06:04,687 --> 00:06:06,231
A video game console?

107
00:06:06,231 --> 00:06:07,776
Almost certainly not.

108
00:06:08,276 --> 00:06:10,401
Even the night would look different.

109
00:06:10,401 --> 00:06:11,404
Cities would hum,

110
00:06:11,404 --> 00:06:15,417
and machine rooms would glow orange from thousands of hot filaments.

111
00:06:18,167 --> 00:06:19,541
But here's the thing.

112
00:06:19,541 --> 00:06:21,046
Tubes never fully died.

113
00:06:21,046 --> 00:06:26,281
Your microwave oven heats food with a magnetron, which is a kind of vacuum tube.

114
00:06:26,281 --> 00:06:29,488
Medical X-ray machines make their rays in a tube.

115
00:06:29,988 --> 00:06:34,568
And plenty of guitarists still swear by tube amplifiers for their warm,

116
00:06:34,568 --> 00:06:35,471
crunchy sound.

117
00:06:36,333 --> 00:06:40,892
In 1976, a Soviet pilot flew a MiG-25 fighter jet to Japan.

118
00:06:40,892 --> 00:06:43,674
When Western analysts took it apart,

119
00:06:43,674 --> 00:06:49,623
they were surprised to find that much of its electronics ran on vacuum tubes.

120
00:06:50,123 --> 00:06:51,021
Old-fashioned?

121
00:06:51,021 --> 00:06:51,405
Maybe.

122
00:06:51,405 --> 00:06:52,687
But tubes are tough.

123
00:06:52,687 --> 00:06:57,750
They can keep working in radiation that would wreck delicate early transistors.

124
00:06:58,250 --> 00:07:00,604
And at NASA's Ames Research Center,

125
00:07:00,604 --> 00:07:04,168
researchers even built a nanoscale vacuum transistor,

126
00:07:04,168 --> 00:07:06,253
a tiny tube carved into a chip,

127
00:07:06,253 --> 00:07:10,355
that reached four hundred and sixty gigahertz in early tests.

128
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:12,465
And here's the twist.

129
00:07:12,465 --> 00:07:15,658
The transistor almost arrived twenty-two years early.

130
00:07:16,158 --> 00:07:16,768
In 1925,

131
00:07:16,768 --> 00:07:26,906
a physicist named Julius Lilienfeld filed a patent in Canada for a device that controlled current in a solid using an electric field.

132
00:07:26,906 --> 00:07:28,888
A field-effect transistor.

133
00:07:29,388 --> 00:07:32,937
As far as anyone knows, he never got a working version.

134
00:07:32,937 --> 00:07:36,293
The ultra-pure materials it needed didn't exist yet.

135
00:07:36,293 --> 00:07:40,746
But when Bell Labs tried to patent its own designs in the late 1940s,

136
00:07:40,746 --> 00:07:44,876
many of the claims were rejected, partly because of his patents.

137
00:07:45,733 --> 00:07:46,850
Then, in 1959,

138
00:07:46,850 --> 00:07:54,744
Mohamed Atalla and Dawon Kahng at Bell Labs built a working field-effect transistor out of silicon,

139
00:07:54,744 --> 00:07:57,455
with a thin layer of glass on top.

140
00:07:57,455 --> 00:07:58,332
The MOSFET.

141
00:07:58,332 --> 00:08:02,877
Much closer to Lilienfeld's idea than to the 1947 device.

142
00:08:03,377 --> 00:08:06,870
It turned out to be the one that took over the world.

143
00:08:06,870 --> 00:08:11,616
By 2018, humanity had made an estimated thirteen sextillion transistors.

144
00:08:11,616 --> 00:08:14,713
That's a thirteen followed by twenty-one zeros,

145
00:08:14,713 --> 00:08:18,799
and about ninety-nine point nine percent of them were MOSFETs.

146
00:08:19,299 --> 00:08:23,472
That makes the transistor the most manufactured thing in human history.

147
00:08:24,333 --> 00:08:27,077
So the transistor wasn't one lucky accident.

148
00:08:27,077 --> 00:08:31,192
The idea waited decades for materials good enough to make it work.

149
00:08:31,192 --> 00:08:34,683
Without it, your world would be orange, hot and humming,

150
00:08:34,683 --> 00:08:38,050
and computers would fill buildings instead of pockets.

151
00:08:38,550 --> 00:08:41,809
Which piece of tech would you miss most in a vacuum-tube world?

152
00:08:41,809 --> 00:08:43,050
Tell me in the comments.
